There is theme within the workbook that may cause issues with the ontology project. This is the common occurrence of the idea of Presumption of Registrability. Justice Branson discusses the concept of the Registrar being ‘satisfied’ which is an expression used in section 33 and 41 (prior to Raising the Bar). In Blount Inc v Registrar of Trade Marks (1998) 40 IPR 498, 503 (‘Oregon’) she said:

"[Section] 33 of the Act ... has, in effect, altered the burden of proof generally so far as acceptance of applications is concerned. Section 33(1) provides that the Registrar must accept the application unless satisfied that the application has not been made in accordance with the Act, or that there are grounds for rejecting it."
 
The presumption of registrability applies to s43. The Registrar must be satisfied that a ground for rejection exists before section 33(3) can be applied to reject the trade mark.  If there is doubt that a connotation exists, then the ground for rejection should not be raised.
 
Office practise considers this is a more wholistic manner. It is not merely sufficient for the examiner to have doubt that a connotation exist, but rather the registrar as a whole. Any individual examiner is delegated by the registrar to examine trade marks. This results in a higher bar for the application of presumption of registrability. It is generally expected that examiner consult widely before accepting a trade mark based on the presumption of registrability. Trade marks maintains SMEs (Subject Matter Experts) on each section of the Trade Marks Act. It would be generally expected that an examiner consult with their team leader and the s43 SMEs before allowing a trade mark to proceed to registration based solely on doubt about if a connotation exists. 
 
The workbook contains information both about the idea of "doubt" and the presumption of registrability however lacks the nuance about the offices approach to what would be considered "doubt" for the purpose of applying the presumption of registrability. 

Another concern I have about what I've seen so far is around the definitions of some of the words. The workbook appears to be exclusively seeing Part 29 of the trade mark examiners manual and has pulled out all its definitions based on this section. 
While not wrong, this provides a limited view on what some of the roles are. High level definitions are needed to provide context to the information. 

